WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:06.570
So welcome Andre, I'm really glad you could join me today.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:08.130
Yeah, thank you for having me.

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:11.170
So a lot of people already know your work in deep learning,

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:14.490
but not everyone knows your personal story.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:16.970
So I'd like to ask, and start by telling us,

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:20.410
how did you end up doing all this work in deep learning?

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.570
Yeah, absolutely.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.690
So I think my first exposure to deep learning

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:26.450
was when I was an undergraduate at the University of Toronto.

00:00:26.450 --> 00:00:28.490
And so Jeff Hinton was there and he was teaching a class

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:29.490
on deep learning.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.410
And at the time it was restricted Boltzmann machines

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.090
trained on MNIST digits.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:35.850
And I just really liked the way Jeff

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.330
talked about training the network,

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.330
the mind of the network.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.330
And he was using these terms.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:43.710
And I just thought there was a flavor of something

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.850
magical happening when this was training on those digits.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:50.050
And so that's my first exposure to it,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:52.050
although I didn't get into it in a lot of detail

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:52.970
at that time.

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.350
And then when I was doing my master's degree

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.930
at the University of British Columbia,

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.250
I took a class with Nando de Freitas,

00:00:58.250 --> 00:00:59.970
and that was, again, on machine learning.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:04.170
The first time I delved deeper into these networks and so on.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.270
And what was interesting is that I

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.450
was very interested in artificial intelligence,

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:10.330
and so I took classes in artificial intelligence.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:11.330
But a lot of what I was seeing there

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:12.730
was just very not satisfying.

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.730
It was a lot of depth-first search,

00:01:14.730 --> 00:01:16.610
breadth-first search, alpha beta pruning,

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.370
and all these things.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.050
And I was not understanding how I was not satisfied.

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:22.550
And so when I was seeing neural networks for the first time,

00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:24.770
in machine learning, which is this term that I

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:27.490
think is more technical and not as well known in kind of,

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:29.210
most people talk about artificial intelligence.

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:31.490
Machine learning was more kind of a technical term,

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:32.490
I would almost say.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:34.890
And so I was dissatisfied with artificial intelligence.

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:36.250
And when I saw machine learning, I

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.510
was like, this is the AI that I want to kind of spend time on.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:40.890
This was really interesting.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:44.130
And that's kind of what took me down those directions,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.550
is that this is kind of almost a new computing paradigm,

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.170
I would say.

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:50.450
Because normally, humans write code,

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:54.130
but here in this case, the optimization writes code.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.410
And so you're creating the input-output specification,

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:58.010
and then you have lots of examples of it.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:01:59.290
And then the optimization writes code,

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.290
and sometimes it can write code better than you.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.450
And so I thought that was just a very new kind of way

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:06.050
of thinking about programming.

00:02:06.050 --> 00:02:08.130
And that's what kind of intrigued me about it.

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:10.090
Then through a word, one of the things

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.570
you've come to be known for is that you are now

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:16.570
the human benchmark for the ImageNet classification

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:17.970
competition.

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:19.210
How did that come about?

00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:20.730
So basically, the ImageNet challenge

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.170
is kind of a, it's sometimes compared

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:24.930
to kind of the World Cup of Computer Vision.

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.970
So a lot of people kind of care about this benchmark.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:29.890
Our error rate kind of goes down over time.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.690
And it was not obvious to me kind of where

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.330
a human would be on the scale.

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.970
And I've done a similar smaller scale experiment on CIFAR-10

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:37.010
data set earlier.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.770
So what I did in CIFAR-10 is I just

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:40.530
was looking at these 32 by 32 images,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.210
and I was trying to classify them myself.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.810
At the time, this was only 10 categories,

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.210
so it was fairly simple to create an interface for it.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.050
And I think I had an error rate of about 6% on that.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.810
And so that was, and then based on what I was seeing

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.970
and how hard the task was, I think I predicted

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:55.930
that the lowest error rate we'd achieve

00:02:55.930 --> 00:02:59.010
would be like, OK, I can't remember the exact numbers.

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.170
I think I guessed like 10%.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:03.490
And we're now down to like 3% or 2% or something crazy.

00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:06.210
So that was my first kind of fun experiment

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.090
of human baseline.

00:03:09.090 --> 00:03:12.090
And I thought it was really important for the same purposes

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.170
that you kind of pointed out in some of your lectures.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:16.770
I mean, you really want that number to understand

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:19.930
how well humans are doing, and so you can compare machine

00:03:19.930 --> 00:03:21.330
learning algorithms to it.

00:03:21.330 --> 00:03:22.930
And for ImageNet, it seemed that there

00:03:22.930 --> 00:03:25.170
was a discrepancy between how important this benchmark was

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:27.370
and how much focus there was on getting a lower number

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.130
and us not understanding even how humans

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:31.650
are doing on this benchmark.

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:33.610
And so I created this JavaScript interface,

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:35.330
and I was showing myself the images.

00:03:35.330 --> 00:03:36.930
And then the problem with ImageNet

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.330
is you don't have just 10 categories, you have 1,000.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.450
And so it was almost like a UI challenge of,

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.370
obviously, I can't remember 1,000 categories.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.410
So how do I make it so that it's something fair?

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:48.050
And so I listed out all the categories,

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:49.410
and I gave myself examples of them.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:51.090
And so for each image, I was scrolling

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:52.650
through 1,000 categories and just trying

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:56.970
to see, based on the examples I was seeing for each category,

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.610
what this image might be.

00:03:58.610 --> 00:04:01.890
And I thought it was just an extremely instructive exercise

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:02.490
by itself.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:05.570
I mean, I did not understand that a third of ImageNet

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:07.410
is dogs and dog species.

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:09.850
And so that was interesting to see

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.010
that the network spends a huge amount of time caring

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:12.890
about dogs, I think.

00:04:12.890 --> 00:04:16.450
A third of its performance comes from dogs.

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.890
And yeah, so this was something that I

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.210
did for maybe a week or two.

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:22.130
I put everything else on hold.

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.250
I thought it was kind of a very fun exercise.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:25.570
I got a number in the end.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:27.210
And then I thought that one person is not enough.

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.890
I wanted to have multiple other people.

00:04:28.890 --> 00:04:31.090
And so I was trying to organize within the lab

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:33.210
to get other people to kind of do the same thing.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.530
And I think people are not as willing to contribute,

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:41.310
say, like a week or two of pretty painstaking work,

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:43.130
just sitting down for five hours

00:04:43.130 --> 00:04:45.370
and trying to figure out which dog breed this is.

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.970
And so I was not able to get enough data in that respect.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.490
But we got at least some approximate performance,

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:53.370
which I thought was fun.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.210
And then this was kind of picked up.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:04:57.090
And it wasn't obvious to me at the time.

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.510
I just wanted to know the number.

00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:00.890
But this became like a thing.

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:03.130
And people really liked the fact that this happened.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.090
And I'm referred to jokingly as like the reference human.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:10.490
And of course, that's kind of hilarious to me.

00:05:10.490 --> 00:05:12.450
Yeah.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:17.130
Were you surprised when software, deep nets, finally

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.890
surpass your performance?

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:20.450
Absolutely.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.130
Yeah, absolutely.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:26.450
I mean, sometimes it's really hard to see in the image

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:26.950
what it is.

00:05:26.950 --> 00:05:29.530
It's just like a tiny blob of like a black dog

00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:30.690
is obviously somewhere there.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:33.890
And I'm guessing between like 20 categories.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:35.090
And the network just gets it.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:37.770
And I don't understand how that comes about.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.410
So there's some super humanness to it.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.610
But also for the, I think the network

00:05:41.610 --> 00:05:43.530
is extremely good at these kind of statistics

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:44.970
of four types and textures.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:48.170
And I think in that respect, I was not

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:49.810
surprised that the network could better

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:54.250
measure those fine statistics across lots of images.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:56.570
In many cases, I was surprised because some of the images

00:05:56.570 --> 00:05:57.610
required you to read.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.610
Like it's just a bottle and you can't see what it is.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:01.570
But it actually tells you what it is in text.

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:03.530
And so as a human, I can read it and it's fine.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:04.650
But the network would have to learn

00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:07.050
to read to identify the object because it wasn't obvious

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.210
just from it.

00:06:09.210 --> 00:06:11.290
One of the things you've become well known for

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.070
and that the deep learning community has been

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:15.250
grateful to you for has been your teaching the Santa

00:06:15.250 --> 00:06:17.490
class and putting that online.

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.090
Tell me about how that came about.

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:20.930
Yeah, absolutely.

00:06:20.930 --> 00:06:26.690
So I think I felt very strongly that basically this technology

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.450
was transformative and that a lot of people want to use it.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:30.610
It's almost like a hammer.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.690
And what I wanted to do, I was in a position

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:36.090
to randomly hand out this hammer to a lot of people.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:37.770
And I just found that very compelling.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:40.370
It's not necessarily advisable from the perspective

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:41.450
of a PhD student because you're

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:42.570
putting your research on hold.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:44.610
This became 120% of my time.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:45.930
And I had to put all of research

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:48.370
on hold for maybe, I mean, I taught the class twice

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:49.970
and each time it's maybe four months.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:52.410
And so that time is basically spent entirely on the class.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.410
So it's not super advisable from that perspective.

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.490
But it was basically the highlight of my PhD.

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:57.810
It's not even like related to research.

00:06:57.810 --> 00:06:59.490
I think teaching the class was definitely

00:06:59.490 --> 00:07:00.970
the highlight of my PhD.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:02.690
Just seeing the students, just the fact

00:07:02.690 --> 00:07:04.370
that they were really excited,

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.010
it was a very different class.

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.550
Normally, you're being taught things

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:09.770
that were discovered in 1800 or something like that.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.130
But we were able to come to class and say,

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:12.970
look, there's this paper from like a week ago

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.050
or even like yesterday.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.050
And there's new results.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:17.690
I think the undergraduate students and the other students,

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:19.730
they just really enjoyed that aspect of the class

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.570
and the fact that they actually understood.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:24.210
So there's not, so you don't have to,

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:26.370
this is not nuclear physics or rocket science.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:27.730
This is like you need to know calculus

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.030
and linear algebra and you can actually kind of understand

00:07:30.030 --> 00:07:31.730
everything that happens under the hood.

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:34.130
And so I think just the fact that it's so powerful,

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.210
the fact that it's, that it keeps changing on a daily basis

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:38.770
and people kind of felt like they're on the forefront

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:39.610
of something big.

00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:42.070
And I think that's why people like really enjoyed

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:42.910
that class a lot.

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.030
And you really helped a lot of people

00:07:46.030 --> 00:07:47.630
and had a lot of hammers.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.950
As someone that's been doing deep learning

00:07:51.950 --> 00:07:56.590
for quite some time now, the field is evolving rapidly.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:57.710
I think Chris is here.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.230
How has your own thinking,

00:07:59.230 --> 00:08:01.310
how has your understanding of deep learning changed

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.470
over these many years?

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:05.710
Yeah, it's basically like when I was seeing

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:08.510
restricted-ballson machines for the first time on digits,

00:08:08.510 --> 00:08:10.230
it wasn't obvious to me how this technology

00:08:10.230 --> 00:08:12.670
was going to be used and how big of a deal it would be.

00:08:12.670 --> 00:08:14.510
And also when I was starting to work in computer vision,

00:08:14.510 --> 00:08:16.150
convolutional networks, they were around,

00:08:16.150 --> 00:08:18.190
but they were not something that a lot of the computer

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:22.470
vision community kind of dissipated using anytime soon.

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:24.130
I think the perception was that this works

00:08:24.130 --> 00:08:27.070
for small cases but would never scale to large images.

00:08:27.070 --> 00:08:30.210
And that was just extremely incorrect.

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.870
And so basically, I'm just surprised by how

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:36.630
general technology is and how good the results are.

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:38.630
That was my largest surprise, I would say.

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.250
And it's not only that, so that's one thing

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:42.730
that it worked so well in, say, like ImageNet.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.570
But the other thing that I think no one saw coming,

00:08:44.570 --> 00:08:46.190
or at least for sure I did not see coming,

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:48.170
is that you can take these pre-trained networks

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:49.930
and that you can transfer, you can fine-tune them

00:08:49.930 --> 00:08:51.370
on arbitrary other tasks.

00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:52.850
Because now you're not just solving ImageNet

00:08:52.850 --> 00:08:54.350
and you need millions of examples.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:57.270
This also happens to be very general feature extractor.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:58.770
And I think that's kind of a second insight

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.170
that I think fewer people saw coming.

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:04.050
And there were these papers that are just like,

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:05.770
here are all the things that people have been working on

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:06.770
in computer vision.

00:09:06.770 --> 00:09:09.290
Scene classification, action recognition,

00:09:09.290 --> 00:09:13.290
object recognition, face attributes, and so on.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.070
And people are just kind of crushing each task

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:17.370
just by fine-tuning the network.

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:22.410
And so that to me was very surprising.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.810
And somehow, I guess, supervised learning

00:09:24.810 --> 00:09:25.850
gets most of the press.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.850
And even though pre-training, fine-tuning,

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:32.530
or transfer learning is actually working very well,

00:09:32.530 --> 00:09:34.850
people seem to talk less about that for some reason.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:36.450
Yeah, exactly.

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:38.010
Yeah, I think what has not worked as much

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.210
is some of these hopes around unsupervised learning, which

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:43.410
I think has kind of been really why a lot of researchers

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:48.250
have gotten into the field in around 2007 and so on.

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:50.850
And I think the promise of that has still not been delivered.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.370
And I think I find that also surprising,

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.010
is that the supervised learning part worked so well.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:59.010
And the unsupervised learning is still kind of in a state

00:09:59.010 --> 00:10:01.450
of a, yeah, it's still not obvious how it's going to be

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:03.610
used or how that's going to work,

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:05.570
even though a lot of people are still deep believers,

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:09.610
I would say, to use the term, in this area.

00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:11.490
So I know that one of the presences

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:14.450
in thinking a lot about the long-term future of AI.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:16.130
Do you want to share your thoughts on that?

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:18.370
So I spent the last maybe a year and a half

00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:20.810
at OpenAI kind of thinking a lot about these topics.

00:10:20.810 --> 00:10:27.850
And it seems to me like the field will kind of split

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.250
into two trajectories.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:32.530
One will be kind of applied AI, which is kind of just

00:10:32.530 --> 00:10:34.690
making these neural networks, training them,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:36.770
mostly with supervised learning, potentially unsupervised

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:39.610
learning, and getting better, say, image recognizers

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:41.130
or something like that.

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:43.010
And I think the other will be kind

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:45.010
of artificial general intelligence directions, which

00:10:45.010 --> 00:10:48.210
is kind of how do you get neural networks that

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:50.930
are entire kind of dynamical system that thinks and speaks

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:52.770
and can do everything that a human can do

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:54.650
as intelligent in that way.

00:10:54.650 --> 00:10:57.130
And I think that what's been interesting is that,

00:10:57.130 --> 00:10:58.530
for example, in computer vision,

00:10:58.530 --> 00:10:59.970
the way we approached it in the beginning, I think,

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:02.010
was wrong, in that we tried to break it down

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:02.970
by different parts.

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:05.050
So we were like, OK, humans recognize people.

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.210
Humans recognize scenes.

00:11:06.210 --> 00:11:07.690
Human recognize objects.

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:10.290
So we're just going to do everything that humans do.

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:11.970
And then once we have all those things,

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.610
and now we have different areas, and once we

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:14.330
have all those things, we're going

00:11:14.330 --> 00:11:16.010
to figure out how to put them together.

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:17.850
And I think that was kind of a wrong approach.

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.050
And we've seen how that kind of played out historically.

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:23.130
And so I think there's something similar that's

00:11:23.130 --> 00:11:25.170
going on slightly on the higher level with AI.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:28.250
So kind of people are asking, well, OK, people plan.

00:11:28.250 --> 00:11:30.850
People do experiments to figure out how the world works.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:32.930
Or people talk to other people, so we need language.

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:35.410
And people are trying to decompose it by function,

00:11:35.410 --> 00:11:37.250
accomplish each piece, and then put it together

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:38.290
into some kind of brain.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:41.370
And I just think it's kind of just an incorrect approach.

00:11:41.370 --> 00:11:43.150
And so what I've been a much bigger fan of

00:11:43.150 --> 00:11:47.970
is not decomposing that way, but having

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.970
a single kind of neural network there

00:11:49.970 --> 00:11:51.170
is the complete dynamical system

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.570
that you're always working with, a full agent.

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:55.370
And then the question is, how do you actually

00:11:55.370 --> 00:11:57.410
create objectives such that when

00:11:57.410 --> 00:12:00.450
you optimize over the weights to make up that brain,

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.250
you get intelligent behavior out?

00:12:02.250 --> 00:12:04.130
And so that's kind of been something

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:05.970
that I've been thinking about a lot at OpenAI.

00:12:05.970 --> 00:12:08.210
I think there are a lot of different ways

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.410
that people have thought about approaching this problem.

00:12:11.410 --> 00:12:13.850
So for example, going in a supervised learning direction,

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:15.430
I have this essay online.

00:12:15.430 --> 00:12:15.970
It's not an essay.

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:17.770
It's kind of a short story that I wrote.

00:12:17.770 --> 00:12:18.810
And the short story kind of tries

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:20.610
to come up with a hypothetical world of what

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:23.610
it might look like if the way we approach this AGI

00:12:23.610 --> 00:12:25.490
is just by scaling up supervised learning, which

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:28.210
we know works.

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.210
And so that gets into something that

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:31.730
looks like Amazon Mechanical Turk, where

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:33.770
people accession to lots of robot bodies,

00:12:33.770 --> 00:12:34.930
and they perform tasks.

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:37.090
And then we train on that as a supervised learning data

00:12:37.090 --> 00:12:40.250
set to imitate humans and what that might look like and so on.

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:42.610
And so then there are other directions,

00:12:42.610 --> 00:12:45.770
like unsupervised learning from algorithmic information theory,

00:12:45.770 --> 00:12:49.170
things like AI, XI, or from artificial life,

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.730
things that look more like artificial evolution.

00:12:51.730 --> 00:12:54.890
And so that's kind of what I spend my time thinking a lot

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:55.450
about.

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.010
And I think I had the correct answer,

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:59.610
but I'm not willing to reveal it here.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.010
But I can at least learn more by reading your blog post.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:03.610
Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:07.650
So you've already given out a lot of hammers.

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:09.810
And today, there are a lot of people

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:13.210
still wanting to enter the field of AI, enter deep learning.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:17.330
So for people in that position, what advice do you have for them?

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:18.330
Yeah, absolutely.

00:13:18.330 --> 00:13:21.570
So I think when people talk to me about CS231n

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:24.250
and why they thought it was a very useful course,

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:26.450
what I keep hearing again and again is just

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.330
people appreciate the fact that we got all the way

00:13:28.330 --> 00:13:29.610
to the low-level details.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:31.210
And they were not working with the library.

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:32.570
They saw the row code.

00:13:32.570 --> 00:13:34.330
And they saw how everything was implemented

00:13:34.330 --> 00:13:36.730
and implemented chunks of it themselves.

00:13:36.730 --> 00:13:39.770
And so just going all the way down to the understanding

00:13:39.770 --> 00:13:41.770
everything under you, and never,

00:13:41.770 --> 00:13:43.970
it's really important to not abstract away things.

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:45.930
Like you need to have a full understanding of the whole

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:46.570
stack.

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:48.410
And that's where I learned the most myself as well.

00:13:48.410 --> 00:13:49.330
When I was learning this stuff,

00:13:49.330 --> 00:13:51.170
it's just implementing it myself from scratch

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:53.210
was the most important.

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.930
It was the piece that I felt gave me

00:13:56.930 --> 00:13:59.370
the best bang for the buck in terms of understanding.

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:00.570
So I wrote my own library.

00:14:00.570 --> 00:14:01.570
It's called Common Edge JS.

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:03.090
It was written in JavaScript and implements

00:14:03.090 --> 00:14:04.010
convolutional neural networks.

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:06.370
That was my way of learning about back propagation.

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.010
And so that's something that I keep advising people

00:14:09.010 --> 00:14:12.810
is that you not work with TensorFlow or something else.

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:15.250
You can work with it once you have written it

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:17.010
something yourself on the lowest detail.

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:18.570
You understand everything under you.

00:14:18.570 --> 00:14:21.570
And now it's possible to use some of these frameworks that

00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:23.010
abstract some of it away from you,

00:14:23.010 --> 00:14:24.650
but you know what's under the hood.

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:26.770
And so that's been something that helped me the most.

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:28.530
That's something that people appreciate the most

00:14:28.530 --> 00:14:29.650
when they take 231n.

00:14:29.650 --> 00:14:31.490
And that's what I would advise a lot of people.

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:33.970
So rather than run it neural networks,

00:14:33.970 --> 00:14:35.490
it will all happen to imagine.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:37.210
Yeah, and it's some kind of a sequence of layers.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:39.130
And I know that when I add some dropout layers,

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:40.210
it makes it work better.

00:14:40.210 --> 00:14:41.810
That's not what you want.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:44.570
In that case, you're not going to be able to debug

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:45.090
effectively.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:47.170
You're not going to be able to improve on models

00:14:47.170 --> 00:14:48.690
effectively.

00:14:48.690 --> 00:14:51.850
With that answer, I'm really glad that the deep learning.ai

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:54.770
course starts off with many weeks of Python programming

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:55.450
for a second.

00:14:55.450 --> 00:14:56.210
And then the framework.

00:14:56.930 --> 00:14:57.450
Good.

00:14:57.450 --> 00:14:58.450
Good.

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:01.610
Thank you very much for sharing your insights and advice.

00:15:01.610 --> 00:15:04.450
You're already a hero to many people in the deep learning

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:04.930
world.

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:08.290
So really glad, really grateful you could join us here today.

00:15:08.290 --> 00:15:10.660
Yeah, thank you for having me.
